  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nIt's an awfully warm and pleasant Saturday out there for the \nsecond week of October. Temperatures early this afternoon are in\nthe upper 70s to lower 80s with a comfortable southerly breeze.\nLargely sunny skies are being filtered by blowoff cirrus from \npost-tropical storm Isaias as it spins northward over the \nsoutheast US. The blowoff will remain overhead into the night \nand gradually retreat from west to east during the day tomorrow.\nThe NBM has now backed off on any mentionable PoPs in our east \nand southeast from this remnant storm for tonight and tomorrow \nmorning. A few pieces of deterministic guidance, as well as a \nhandful of members from various ensembles, still suggest a brief\nlight shower may be possible. Accordingly, maintained some \n\"silent\" (less than 15%) PoPs in our east in the gridded \nforecast.\n\nTomorrow should see highs ranging from the lower to middle 70s \nin our east to near 80 in our west and southwest. It's worth \nnoting that afternoon temperatures from the NBM in the Sunday \nthrough Tuesday period are being skewed by a warm GEPS suite, so\nhave collaborated with neighboring offices to bring temps down \ncloser to most other guidance. Being on the southern flank of a \npassing low on Monday, efficient warm advection driven by gusty \nsoutherly winds will pull highs into the 80s, possibly even \nnearing 90 closer to central IL. Near-record-breaking values are\nforecast at Chicago and Rockford. Tuesday will feature a good \nspread in temps across the area with a cold frontal passage \nduring the day and should range from the 70s north into the 80s \nsouth. High temperatures are then favored to fall through the \nrest of the week and conditions by next weekend should feel much\nmore like what we normally expect for the middle of October.\n\nStill eyeing a signal for a surface low to track across the \nupper Midwest and drag a cold front across the area late Monday \ninto Tuesday. Recently, however, global guidance has backed off \na bit on PoPs with the better forcing and moisture favored just \nto our north and northeast ahead of the low and a weakly-forced,\nmoisture-starved cold front as it moves across on Tuesday. A \nlow chance for some showers and perhaps a thunderstorm still \nexists late Monday and into Tuesday, and should be maximized \naround our northeastern CWA. Regular chances for rain are then \nin the forecast from late Tuesday into Friday as the regional \nsynoptic pattern becomes a bit more active for next week.\n\nDoom",
